'''acoustics''' (ạ-kös′- or ạ-kous′tiks), ''n.'' [Pl. of ''acoustic'' (see ''-ics''); = F. ''acoustique'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''acustica.''] The science of sound; the study of the cause, nature, and phenomena of the vibrations of elastic bodies which affect the organ of hearing. {{smaller|The manner in which sound is produced, its transmission through air and other media (sometimes called ''diacoustics''), the theory of reflected sound, or echoes (sometimes called ''catacoustics''), the properties and effects of different sounds, including musical sounds or notes, and the structure and action of the organ of hearing, are all included in acoustics. See ''sound.''}}

